    {
      "step_id": "tile_sql_adv_execution_order_subquery_where.s06",
      "type": "explainer",
      "concept_ids": [
        "sql.subquery_in_where"
      ],
      "eyebrow": "Subqueries in WHERE",
      "title": "Using a query result as a filter value",
      "lede": "When the filter value itself must be computed — like the average delivery time — you embed a query inside WHERE.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "block_id": "tile_sql_adv_execution_order_subquery_where.s06.b1",
          "role": "intro",
          "text": "Suppose you want the orders that took longer than the average delivery time across all orders. You don't know that average ahead of time — it has to be computed by another query, used right inside the WHERE clause.",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "sql.subquery_in_where"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "sql.subquery_in_where#tb1"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "sql.subquery_in_where#f2"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "block_id": "tile_sql_adv_execution_order_subquery_where.s06.b2",
          "role": "definition",
          "text": "A subquery that returns a single (scalar) value can be used as a value expression in a comparison. SELECT order_id, delivery_time_minutes FROM orders WHERE delivery_time_minutes > (SELECT AVG(delivery_time_minutes) FROM orders) keeps only the orders slower than the overall average — the inner SELECT computes that one number, and the outer WHERE compares each row's delivery time against it.",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "sql.subquery_in_where"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "sql.subquery_in_where#tb2"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "sql.subquery_in_where#f2"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "block_id": "tile_sql_adv_execution_order_subquery_where.s06.b3",
          "role": "mechanism",
          "text": "A subquery can also appear as the right side of IN: WHERE customer_id IN (SELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE delivery_time_minutes > 40) keeps rows whose customer_id value is found in the subquery's result. SELECT name FROM customers WHERE customer_id IN (SELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE delivery_time_minutes > 40) finds the customers who have placed at least one order slower than 40 minutes.",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "sql.subquery_in_where"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "sql.subquery_in_where#tb3"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "sql.subquery_in_where#f1"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code_walkthrough",
          "block_id": "tile_sql_adv_execution_order_subquery_where.s06.b4",
          "language": "sql",
          "code": "-- Scalar subquery: inner query returns one value\nSELECT order_id, delivery_time_minutes\nFROM orders\nWHERE delivery_time_minutes > (\n    SELECT AVG(delivery_time_minutes)\n    FROM orders\n);\n\n-- IN subquery: inner query returns a list of values\nSELECT name\nFROM customers\nWHERE customer_id IN (\n    SELECT customer_id\n    FROM orders\n    WHERE delivery_time_minutes > 40\n);",
          "annotations": [
            {
              "start_line": 4,
              "end_line": 6,
              "text": "Inner query: computes one average value"
            },
            {
              "start_line": 12,
              "end_line": 15,
              "text": "Inner query: returns a list of customer_ids with slow orders"
            }
          ],
          "reveal": "all",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "sql.subquery_in_where"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "sql.subquery_in_where#tb2",
